## Logical Relationships

| Relationship | Justification |
|--------------|---------------|
| **G1 → G2, G3**  (AND, `;`) | G1 requires that the robot first collects resources (G2) and then delivers them (G3) in a sequential order. |
| **G2 → G2.1, G2.2**  (OR, `FALLBACK`) | Collection can succeed either via a normal sequence (G2.1) or by handling a battery‑low situation (G2.2). |
| **G2.1 → AT1, AT2, AT3**  (AND, `;`) | The normal collection sequence consists of navigating, requesting, and waiting for the resource, executed one after the other. |
| **G2.2 → AT4, AT5**  (AND, `;`) | When the battery is low during collection, the robot must first recharge (AT4) and then assign the remaining mission to another robot (AT5). |
| **G3 → G3.1, G3.2**  (OR, `FALLBACK`) | Delivery can succeed either via a normal sequence (G3.1) or by handling a battery‑low situation (G3.2). |
| **G3.1 → AT6, AT7**  (AND, `;`) | The normal delivery sequence consists of navigating to the delivery location (AT6) and then delivering the resource (AT7). |
| **G3.2 → AT8, AT5, G4**  (AND, `;`) | When the battery is low during delivery, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint (AT8), assign the remaining task to another robot (AT5), and, if the return fails, trigger failure handling (G4). |
| **G4 → AT9, AT10**  (AND, `;`) | Failure handling consists of triggering an alert (AT9) and sending a report to the sector manager (AT10). |

